@* Combobox's Overview, written by hand after Select's: typing to filter, searching on the
   server previewed in each mode, a required combobox with a placeholder, sizes
   and states, and its own words. Accessibility and See also come from the doc comment. *@

<Heading Level="2">Usage</Heading>
<Code Block Language="razor">@Usage</Code>
<TextBlock>A combobox is a text field with a list of options under it: the user types to narrow the list, then picks one. It suits long lists the user finds an option in by typing. For a short list, see <Anchor Href="docs/components/select">Select</Anchor>, which is simpler. For several choices, see <Anchor Href="docs/components/multi-select">MultiSelect</Anchor>.</TextBlock>

<Heading Level="2">Typing to filter</Heading>
<TextBlock>As the user types, the list shows only the options that contain the text, ignoring case and accents. Pass the options to <Code>Items</Code>, and set <Code>ItemText</Code> to the text shown for each.</TextBlock>
<ExampleViewer Example="combobox-filtering" />
<TextBlock>Typing an option's whole text and leaving the field chooses it, so keep each <Code>ItemText</Code> unique. When you bind an enum, its values are the options, named by their <Code>[Display(Name)]</Code>, and <Code>Items</Code> isn't needed.</TextBlock>

<Heading Level="2">Searching a long list</Heading>
<TextBlock>For a list too long to send to the browser whole, the combobox can ask your code for the options that match what's typed. Set <Code>Search</Code> to a method that receives the typed text and returns the first matching values, of the bound type. To add markup to each option, such as an avatar, set <Code>ItemTemplate</Code>.</TextBlock>
<ExampleViewer Example="combobox-server-search" />
<List>
    <ListItem><Code>Search</Code> is called with "" to fill the list on every page. On <Anchor Href="docs/common/render-modes">interactive pages</Anchor> it's called again as the user types, <Code>Debounce</Code> milliseconds after the last key (250 by default).</ListItem>
    <ListItem>On static pages, the browser filters that first list. Return what the user is most likely to want, or put the page in an interactive render mode.</ListItem>
    <ListItem><Code>Search</Code> runs where the component does: on the server, or in the browser on WebAssembly, where it calls your API.</ListItem>
</List>
<TextBlock>The field shows <Code>ItemText</Code> for the chosen value even when the results don't include it. So <Code>ItemText</Code> receives any value that's bound or posted: look it up without throwing, as the examples do. Before trusting a posted value, see <Anchor Href="docs/common/forms#values-a-form-post-can-change">Values a form post can change</Anchor>.</TextBlock>

<Heading Level="2">Required, with a placeholder</Heading>
<TextBlock>A required combobox starts empty, showing its placeholder, and the form doesn't post until the user picks an option. Bind a nullable property with <Code>[Required]</Code>, and set <Code>Placeholder</Code>.</TextBlock>
<ExampleViewer Example="combobox-required" />
<TextBlock>The property must be nullable. An <Code>int</Code> or an enum always has a value (0 or the first member), so the placeholder never shows, and emptying the field is an error.</TextBlock>

<Heading Level="2">Sizes, read-only and disabled</Heading>
<TextBlock>The size lines the combobox up with buttons and other fields; set <Code>Size</Code> to choose one. For a value the user can see but not change, set <Code>ReadOnly</Code>; it still posts. For a value that doesn't post, set <Code>Disabled</Code>.</TextBlock>
<ExampleViewer Example="combobox-states" />
<TextBlock>HTML has no read-only select, so a read-only combobox shows its choice in a read-only text field and posts it from a hidden input. Before trusting a posted value, see <Anchor Href="docs/common/forms#values-a-form-post-can-change">Values a form post can change</Anchor>. Before saving a disabled value from a form post, see <Anchor Href="docs/common/forms#disabled-fields-and-form-posts">Disabled fields and form posts</Anchor>.</TextBlock>

<Heading Level="2">Labels and localization</Heading>
<TextBlock>The combobox shows text when nothing matches or a search is running, and its toggle button has a name. To set them for the whole app, see <Anchor Href="docs/common/text">Text and other languages</Anchor>.</TextBlock>
<Prose Class="site-table ib-scroll">
    <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Parameter</th><th>Sets</th><th>Default</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
            <tr><td><Code>EmptyText</Code></td><td>The text when no option matches</td><td>"No matches"</td></tr>
            <tr><td><Code>SearchingText</Code></td><td>The text while <Code>Search</Code> runs</td><td>"Searching…"</td></tr>
            <tr><td><Code>ToggleLabel</Code></td><td>The name of the button that opens the list</td><td>"Show options"</td></tr>
        </tbody>
    </table>
</Prose>
<TextBlock>Type "zzz" in the first example to see "No matches".</TextBlock>

<Heading Level="2">Mode support</Heading>
<ModeSupportTable Component="Component" ApiComponent="ApiComponent" />

@if (ApiComponent.Accessibility is not null)
{
    <Heading Level="2">Accessibility</Heading>
    <DocHtml Html="@ApiComponent.Accessibility" Context="@(Context with { Overview = true })" Size="ProseSize.Md" />
}

<Heading Level="2">Parameters</Heading>
<ParameterTable ApiComponent="ApiComponent" ApiHref="@Registry.ApiPageHref(Component)" Context="Context" Compact />

<OverviewSeeAlso Component="Component" ApiComponent="ApiComponent" />

@code {
    [Parameter, EditorRequired] public RegistryComponent Component { get; set; } = null!;

    [Parameter, EditorRequired] public ApiComponent ApiComponent { get; set; } = null!;

    [Parameter, EditorRequired] public ApiPageContext Context { get; set; } = null!;

    private const string Usage = """
        @* A list people filter by typing. *@
        <Combobox @bind-Value="Model!.Country" Items="Countries.Keys"
                  ItemText="code => Countries.GetValueOrDefault(code!, code!)"
                  Placeholder="Search countries" />

        @* A list searched as you type, by your code. *@
        <Combobox @bind-Value="Model!.OwnerId" Search="FindPeople"
                  ItemText="id => People.GetValueOrDefault(id!.Value)?.Name ?? id.ToString()!" />
        """;
}
